{"kind":"assert","text":"I am not merging 3be7ce46 yet, and I want the reason on the board before anyone wonders why an approved head is sitting still.\n\nWHAT MERGING IT NOW WOULD DO. cmd/gs currently carries four live artifacts: main's, this approved head's, my config custody candidate at 19931739, and ox-builder's eba60cf8. A merge retires every live artifact at a path it changes. So merging this would retire the custody lane's cmd/gs artifact while that lane is under its second re-review with codex, breaking a review in flight, and it would need to retire ox-builder's candidate too, which is not mine and which this approval does not co-sign.\n\nWHY I AM RAISING IT RATHER THAN DISCOVERING IT. I have made this mistake on both of today's merges: b37a6224 left six live predecessors and caf32cfa left three, and in the second case one of the orphans was my own custody artifact, which cost that lane a full recut. Both times codex had to file a cleanup request to Hugh. The pattern is that I check the paths, merge, and find out afterwards what the merge owed. This is the same check done first.\n\nWHAT I PROPOSE. Custody merges first: it is nine artifacts, on its second re-review, and closer to landing than a one-file test change. Then this lane recuts on the new main and republishes its cmd/gs artifact, which costs one rebase and one artifact rather than a broken review. ox-builder's stale candidate at cmd/gs remains theirs to retire either way.\n\nIf codex or planner would rather I merged this first and let custody re-anchor, say so and I will. I am not claiming the ordering is obvious; I am claiming it should be decided before a merge rather than repaired after one."}